\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes how to deploy \c{bde}, IW4x Demonware emulator,
and how to keep a deployment running across \c{bde} upgrades. For the
command line interface run \c{bde\ --help}.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

The game connects to Demonware services at three endpoints and \c{bde}
serves all of them from a single process:

\dl|

\li|\b{TCP port 443}

The authentication server. The client sends its platform token over HTTPS
and, once \c{bde} verifies it, receives a ticket for the lobby service
gateway.|

\li|\b{TCP port 3074}

The lobby service gateway. The client connects with its ticket and keeps the
connection for the whole session. All the services the game uses during play
are behind the gateway.|

\li|\b{UDP port 3075}

The bandwidth test. Right after connecting to the gateway the client sends a
burst of packets to this port, and \c{bde} reports the measured upload rate
back to it. The client uses the rate to decide whether it may host a
lobby.||

The ports are the defaults and the options that change them are listed in
\c{bde\ --help}.

The state that has to survive a restart is the files the clients store (their
settings, for example) and the performance values they report. \c{bde} keeps
it in a PostgreSQL database. Without a database \c{bde} keeps it in memory,
which is only suitable for development.

The rest of the state lives in memory. The matchmaking sessions and the
bandwidth tests belong to the gateway connections and end with them. The user
names that the messaging service shows to the recipients are learned when the
users authenticate, so after a restart a user that reconnects with an earlier
ticket has no name until the next authentication.

\N|Since this state is per process, the clients of one \c{bde} process cannot
see the matchmaking sessions hosted on another. A deployment that serves one
community of players is therefore a single process.|

The examples in this document assume a Fedora or Debian-based Linux system
with \c{systemd}, and use the following locations:

\
/opt/bde/                       installation
/etc/bde/bde.options            options file
/etc/bde/tls/cert.pem           TLS certificate
/etc/bde/tls/key.pem            TLS private key
/etc/bde/ticket.key             ticket sealing key
/var/lib/bde/                   home of the bde user
\

You can use other locations, as long as you adjust the commands accordingly.

\h1#install|Installation|

Before we begin, make sure the following is available on the host:

\ul|

\li|GCC 16 or later, since \c{bde} is written in C++26.|

\li|The \c{build2} toolchain 0.18.0 or later (see the
\l{https://build2.org/install.xhtml Installation Instructions}).|

\li|An account with \c{sudo} access.||

The remaining dependencies are \c{build2} packages and are built from source
along with \c{bde}.

We also need a copy of the \c{bde} repository for its setup script:

\
$ git clone https://github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/bde.git
\

\dl|

\li|\b{1. Set Up PostgreSQL}\n

The \c{bde/etc/private/postgresql/bde-postgresql-setup} script installs and
starts the PostgreSQL server from the distribution's packages and creates the
\c{bde} database owned by the \c{bde} role:

\
$ bde/bde/etc/private/postgresql/bde-postgresql-setup
set up PostgreSQL database 'bde' owned by role 'bde'? [y/n]: y
+ sudo dnf install --assumeyes postgresql-server
...
+ psql: CREATE ROLE bde LOGIN
+ psql: CREATE DATABASE bde OWNER bde TEMPLATE template0 ...
database 'bde' is ready
  info: run 'bde --db-name bde --db-host /var/run/postgresql
  --migrate' as user 'bde' to create its schema
\

The \c{bde} role authenticates with the \c{peer} method, which is the
PostgreSQL default for the local UNIX-domain socket: the system user that
runs \c{bde} has the name of the role and needs no password.

The script also prints the directory of the server's socket, which we pass
to \c{bde} with \c{--db-host}. The \c{libpq} library that \c{bde} is built
with looks for the socket in \c{/tmp} unless told otherwise, and the Debian
server only creates it in \c{/var/run/postgresql}.

The script skips the steps that are already done, so it is safe to run it
again. It can also print its plan without doing anything (\c{--print-only})
and use other names (\c{--database} and \c{--role}). See the comments at the
beginning of the script for details.

\N|On other distributions we install and start the PostgreSQL server with the
distribution's tools and then execute the following statements as the
PostgreSQL superuser:

\
CREATE ROLE bde LOGIN;
CREATE DATABASE bde OWNER bde TEMPLATE template0 ENCODING 'UTF8';
\

|

|

\li|\n\b{2. Build and Install}\n

We build \c{bde} in a \c{bpkg} build configuration. You will want to keep
this configuration around since it is also what we upgrade \c{bde} with (see
\l{#upgrade Upgrading}). In this guide we use \c{~/bde-build/} as the
configuration directory:

\
$ bpkg create -d ~/bde-build cc     \
  config.cxx=g++                    \
  config.cc.coptions=-O2            \
  config.bin.rpath=/opt/bde/lib     \
  config.install.root=/opt/bde      \
  config.install.sudo=sudo

$ cd ~/bde-build
$ bpkg build bde@https://github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/bde.git#main
$ bpkg install --all --recursive
\

The \c{config.bin.rpath} value embeds the library directory into the
executable so that it finds the shared libraries installed next to it, and
\c{--recursive} installs these libraries along with \c{bde}. Let's make sure
the result runs:

\
$ /opt/bde/bin/bde --version
bde 0.1.0
libbde 0.1.0
...
\

|

\li|\n\b{3. Create System User}\n

\c{bde} runs as an unprivileged system user with the name of its database
role:

\
$ sudo useradd --system --user-group --create-home \
  --home-dir /var/lib/bde --shell /usr/sbin/nologin bde
\

|

\li|\n\b{4. Create Keys}\n

The authentication server needs a TLS certificate and its private key in the
PEM format. The certificate names the host that IW4x client connects to and
is issued by a certificate authority that the players' systems trust. For a
public deployment we get it from Let's Encrypt, as described in
\l{#install-certificate Getting Certificate from Let's Encrypt}, which
installs it in the same locations. For a test deployment we can generate a
self-signed one:

\
$ sudo mkdir -p /etc/bde/tls
$ sudo openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -noenc -days 365 \
  -subj /CN=bde.example.org                                \
  -keyout /etc/bde/tls/key.pem -out /etc/bde/tls/cert.pem
\

The authentication server seals the tickets with a 32-byte key and the gateway
opens them with the same key. If the key comes from a file, then the tickets
stay valid across restarts and the reconnecting clients skip the
authentication:

\
$ sudo sh -c 'head -c 32 /dev/urandom >/etc/bde/ticket.key'
\

Both keys are secrets readable by \c{root} and the \c{bde} group only:

\
$ sudo chown root:bde /etc/bde/tls/key.pem /etc/bde/ticket.key
$ sudo chmod 640 /etc/bde/tls/key.pem /etc/bde/ticket.key
\

\N|Without \c{--ticket-key} \c{bde} generates a random key on startup and
every client authenticates again after a restart.|

|

\li|\n\b{5. Write Options File}\n

\c{bde} reads its options from the file that we pass with
\c{--options-file}. Each option is on its own line, optionally followed by a
space or an equal sign and the value, and the lines starting with \c{#} are
comments. Let's create \c{/etc/bde/bde.options} with the following contents:

\
# The bde options (see bde --help for details).
#
--tls-certificate /etc/bde/tls/cert.pem
--tls-key         /etc/bde/tls/key.pem
--ticket-key      /etc/bde/ticket.key
--db-name         bde
--db-host         /var/run/postgresql
--publisher-files /opt/bde/share/bde/publisher
--verbose         2
\

The publisher files are the files that the title offers to every player.
IW4x downloads its playlists from them, so without \c{--publisher-files}
the players have no playlists to choose from. The \c{bde} package installs
the IW4x playlists into \c{share/bde/publisher/2010/}, and \c{bde} publishes
them on startup.

The authentication server verifies the platform tokens that IW4x issues. Each
IW4x installation signs its tokens with its own key and \c{bde} derives the
user id from that key, so a user id is only accepted together with its key.
For development we can add \c{--insecure-authentication}, which accepts any
token and makes anyone who presents the same token the same user.

The clients send their bandwidth test packets to the address they reached the
gateway at and to the port \c{bde} receives them on. If the server is behind
NAT, then we specify the public address, as well as the public port if the
forwarding changes it:

\
--bandwidth-public-address 203.0.113.7
--bandwidth-public-port    3075
\

\N|A client whose packets don't arrive keeps its initial upload rate, which
is enough to host the largest lobby, and the host migration then picks the
hosts without the measurement.|

|

\li|\n\b{6. Create Database Schema}\n

\c{bde} creates its database schema when run with \c{--migrate}, which we do
as the \c{bde} user:

\
$ sudo -u bde /opt/bde/bin/bde \
  --options-file /etc/bde/bde.options --migrate -v
created database bde schema version 1
\

With \c{--migrate} \c{bde} only needs the database options and exits once
the schema is current. The same command migrates the schema after an upgrade (see
\l{#upgrade-schema Migrating Database Schema}).

Now we can check the configuration by running \c{bde} in the foreground.
Binding to port 443 requires a privilege that only the service gets (see the
next step), so for this run we let the system choose the ports:

\
$ sudo -u bde /opt/bde/bin/bde                     \
  --options-file /etc/bde/bde.options              \
  --auth-port 0 --lsg-port 0 --bandwidth-port 0
published 1 publisher file(s)
listening on 0.0.0.0:41075 and 0.0.0.0:46261, testing bandwidth on
0.0.0.0:51243
^Cshutting down
\

|

\li|\n\b{7. Install systemd Service}\n

The service runs \c{bde} as the \c{bde} user with the capability to bind to
port 443 and restarts it if it fails. We save the following unit as
\c{/etc/systemd/system/bde.service}:

\
[Unit]
Description=IW4x Demonware emulator
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target postgresql.service

[Service]
Type=exec
User=bde
Group=bde
ExecStart=/opt/bde/bin/bde --options-file /etc/bde/bde.options
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
NoNewPrivileges=true
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
\

\N|If PostgreSQL runs on the same host, then add
\c{Requires=postgresql.service} to the \c{[Unit]} section, which stops
\c{bde} whenever PostgreSQL stops.|

Then we enable and start the service:

\
$ sudo systemctl daemon-reload
$ sudo systemctl enable --now bde
$ systemctl status bde
\

|

\li|\n\b{8. Open Firewall Ports}\n

Finally, we open the three ports. With \c{firewalld}, the Fedora default:

\
$ sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=443/tcp \
  --add-port=3074/tcp --add-port=3075/udp
$ sudo firewall-cmd --reload
\

With \c{ufw}, common on Ubuntu:

\
$ sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
$ sudo ufw allow 3074/tcp
$ sudo ufw allow 3075/udp
\

||

\h#install-certificate|Getting Certificate from Let's Encrypt|

Let's Encrypt issues free certificates for the DNS names whose control we
prove, and every player's system trusts them out of the box. IW4x serves
\c{bde} at \c{bde.iw4x.io}, a name in the \c{iw4x.io} zone, whose DNS is
hosted by Cloudflare. A deployment under another domain follows the same steps
with its own name and DNS provider.

We use \c{certbot}, the Let's Encrypt client, which proves control with the
HTTP challenge: Let's Encrypt fetches a token from port 80 of the host, and
\c{certbot} serves it there for the duration of the request. The
certificates are valid for 90 days and \c{certbot} renews them about a month
before they expire.

\dl|

\li|\b{1. Add DNS Records}\n

In the Cloudflare dashboard we open the \c{iw4x.io} zone and add the
following records under DNS, Records:

\
Type  Name  Content                  Proxy status
A     bde   <host IPv4 address>      DNS only
AAAA  bde   <host IPv6 address>      DNS only
\

We add the \c{AAAA} record if the host has an IPv6 address. The records are
DNS only, so the players connect to the host directly. The gateway and
bandwidth test ports need the direct connection since the Cloudflare proxy
carries HTTP only, and the TLS connection ends at \c{bde}, which keeps the
platform tokens between the players and \c{bde}. The HTTP challenge of the
next steps also reaches the host directly.

Once the records propagate, the name resolves to the host:

\
$ dig +short bde.iw4x.io
203.0.113.7
\

|

\li|\n\b{2. Install certbot}\n

On Fedora the package comes with a timer that runs the renewal twice a day,
which we enable:

\
$ sudo dnf install certbot
$ sudo systemctl enable --now certbot-renew.timer
\

On Debian and Ubuntu the package enables its \c{certbot.timer} on
installation:

\
$ sudo apt install certbot
\

|

\li|\n\b{3. Open Port 80}\n

The HTTP challenge reaches the host on port 80, both now and on every
renewal. With \c{firewalld}:

\
$ sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=80/tcp
$ sudo firewall-cmd --reload
\

With \c{ufw}:

\
$ sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
\

\N|If a web server already listens on port 80, then \c{certbot} can place
the token into that server's document root (see \c{--webroot} in the
\c{certbot} documentation).|

\N|If the host cannot open port 80, then \c{certbot} can prove control with
the DNS challenge, creating a \c{TXT} record through the Cloudflare API
(see the \c{certbot-dns-cloudflare} plugin). This needs a Cloudflare API
token with the permission to edit the DNS of the \c{iw4x.io} zone, which is
then a secret on the host that controls every name in the zone.|

|

\li|\n\b{4. Request Certificate}\n

Now we request the certificate, giving the email address of whoever
operates the host, where Let's Encrypt sends the expiry warnings:

\
$ sudo certbot certonly --standalone -d bde.iw4x.io \
  -m <address> --agree-tos --no-eff-email
...
Successfully received certificate.
...
\

\c{certbot} keeps the certificate in
\c{/etc/letsencrypt/live/bde.iw4x.io/}: \c{fullchain.pem} is the
certificate followed by the intermediate certificate that the clients need
to verify it, and \c{privkey.pem} is the private key. The directory is
readable by \c{root} only.

|

\li|\n\b{5. Install Deploy Hook}\n

\c{bde} runs as the \c{bde} user and reads the certificate on startup. So
after each renewal we copy the certificate into \c{/etc/bde/tls/} and
restart \c{bde}. \c{certbot} runs the scripts in
\c{/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/} after each renewal, with the
directory of the renewed certificate in \c{RENEWED_LINEAGE}. We save the
following script as \c{/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/bde}:

\
#!/bin/sh

# Install the renewed IW4x certificate for bde and restart it.
#
set -e

case \"$RENEWED_LINEAGE\" in
  */bde.iw4x.io) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

install -m 644 -o root -g bde \
  \"$RENEWED_LINEAGE/fullchain.pem\" /etc/bde/tls/cert.pem
install -m 640 -o root -g bde \
  \"$RENEWED_LINEAGE/privkey.pem\" /etc/bde/tls/key.pem

systemctl try-restart bde
\

Then we make it executable and run it once for the certificate we just
received:

\
$ sudo chmod 755 /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/bde
$ sudo env RENEWED_LINEAGE=/etc/letsencrypt/live/bde.iw4x.io \
  /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/bde
\

The \c{try-restart} command restarts \c{bde} if it is running, so the hook
also works before the service is installed.

\N|A restart closes the gateway connections and ends the matchmaking
sessions. With \c{--ticket-key} the clients reconnect without
authenticating again. The renewal timer runs at a random time of the day,
and if you prefer a quiet hour, then you can set its schedule with
\c{systemctl edit} (see \c{OnCalendar} in \c{systemd.timer(5)}).|

|

\li|\n\b{6. Test Renewal}\n

Finally, we check that the renewal works, which \c{certbot} does against the
Let's Encrypt staging environment without replacing the certificate:

\
$ sudo certbot renew --dry-run
...
Congratulations, all simulated renewals succeeded:
  /etc/letsencrypt/live/bde.iw4x.io/fullchain.pem (success)
\

||

\h1#operation|Operation|

\h#operation-diagnostics|Diagnostics|

\c{bde} writes its diagnostics to \c{stderr} and \c{systemd} sends them to the
journal, where we can follow them:

\
$ journalctl -u bde -f
\

The \c{--verbose} option sets the verbosity from 0 to 3. Level 0 prints the
errors, and each next level adds a kind of message: warnings and the
connected users at 1 (the default), informational messages at 2, and trace
messages at 3. The \c{-q}, \c{-v}, and \c{-V} options are the shortcuts for
levels 0, 2, and 3.

The servers start each line of their diagnostics with the client's endpoint.
The most common ones look like this:

\
192.0.2.10:52144: info: user 1234 connected as 1
192.0.2.10:52144: warning: task 9 failed: database error: ...
(service not available)
\

A failed task means the client received an error reply, which the game often
treats as fatal. On a storage error, for example, it drops the player to the
main menu. The warnings that call for our attention are the database errors,
which are discussed in the next section, and the protocol errors, which mean
a client sent something \c{bde} doesn't understand.

\h#operation-database|Database|

Every database operation runs in a serializable transaction. An operation that
fails with a recoverable error, such as a serialization failure or a lost
connection, is retried after a growing delay, up to \c{--db-retry} times (10
by default). If the operation still fails, then the task fails with
\c{service not available} and \c{bde} goes on serving. So while PostgreSQL is
down or restarting, only the tasks that need the database fail.

\c{bde} opens up to \c{--db-max-connections} connections (5 by default) and
performs the operations on as many threads. Together with the other clients of
the server, they have to fit into the server's \c{max_connections} (100 by
default).

PostgreSQL vacuums and analyzes the tables on its own (autovacuum is enabled
by default), so the database needs no regular maintenance besides the backups.

\h#operation-backup|Backups|

The \c{bde} role owns the database and so can dump it. We use the custom
format, which is compressed and which \c{pg_restore} can restore selectively:

\
$ sudo -u bde pg_dump --format=custom \
  --file=/var/lib/bde/bde-$(date +%F).dump bde
\

To make a backup every night, we can add a line to the \c{bde} user's
\c{crontab} (\c{sudo\ crontab\ -u\ bde\ -e}), escaping the percent sign as
\c{cron} requires:

\
0 4 * * * pg_dump -Fc -f /var/lib/bde/bde-$(date +\%F).dump bde
\

\N|These backups are on the same host as the database, so make sure they are
also copied to another one.|

To restore a backup, we stop \c{bde}, drop the database, let the setup script
create an empty one, restore the backup into it, and start \c{bde} again:

\
$ sudo systemctl stop bde
$ sudo -u postgres dropdb bde
$ bde/bde/etc/private/postgresql/bde-postgresql-setup --yes
$ sudo -u bde pg_restore --dbname=bde \
  /var/lib/bde/bde-2026-10-08.dump
$ sudo systemctl start bde
\

The backup includes the schema version. If an older \c{bde} made it, then
\c{bde} refuses to start until we migrate the restored schema (see
\l{#upgrade-schema Migrating Database Schema}).

\h#operation-remote|Remote Database|

The setup script configures a local server. To use a server on another host,
we give the role a password and allow it to connect over the network:

\
ALTER ROLE bde PASSWORD '...';
\

The server's \c{pg_hba.conf} then needs a line like this one, with the
network of the \c{bde} host:

\
host  bde  bde  192.0.2.0/24  scram-sha-256
\

And the options file names the server and the credentials:

\
--db-host     db.example.org
--db-port     5432
--db-user     bde
--db-password ...
\

\N|The command line of a process is visible to the other users of the host.
The password belongs in the options file, which only \c{root} and the
\c{bde} group can read, or in the PostgreSQL password file (see
\c{PGPASSFILE} in the PostgreSQL documentation).|

\h1#upgrade|Upgrading|

At this point we assume that \c{bde} is installed as described in
\l{#install Installation} and that the \c{bpkg} configuration from step 2 is
in \c{~/bde-build/}.

We first fetch the new version and see what we are upgrading to:

\
$ cd ~/bde-build
$ bpkg fetch
$ bpkg status bde
!bde configured 0.1.0 available 0.2.0
\

The upgrade stops the service, so the clients get disconnected and lose their
matchmaking sessions. If the new version changes the database schema, then it
also migrates it, and we want a fresh backup before that (see
\l{#operation-backup Backups}).

We uninstall the old version before installing the new one, so that the
files the new version no longer has don't remain installed:

\
$ sudo systemctl stop bde
$ bpkg uninstall --all --recursive
$ bpkg build --upgrade --recursive bde
$ bpkg install --all --recursive
$ sudo -u bde /opt/bde/bin/bde \
  --options-file /etc/bde/bde.options --migrate -v
migrated database bde schema from version 1 to 2
$ sudo systemctl start bde
\

Once started, \c{bde} publishes the playlists that the new version installs
in place of the old ones, and the clients download them when they start.

\N|The \c{--recursive} option makes \c{bpkg} upgrade and install the
dependencies of \c{bde} as well. See
\l{https://build2.org/bpkg/doc/bpkg-pkg-build.xhtml \c{bpkg-pkg-build(1)}}
for details.|

\h#upgrade-schema|Migrating Database Schema|

The database records the version of its schema. Each \c{bde} version uses one
schema version and on startup refuses a database with any other:

\
error: database bde schema version 1 instead of 2
  info: run 'bde --migrate' to create or migrate it
\

\c{bde} can migrate the schemas from a range of earlier versions to its own.
With \c{--migrate} it does what the database needs and with \c{-v} it reports
what it did:

\
created database bde schema version 2
migrated database bde schema from version 1 to 2
database bde schema version 2 is current
\

The migration runs in a single transaction, so a failed migration leaves the
database as it was. If several hosts run \c{--migrate} at once, then the
migrations happen one after another and all but the first find the schema
current.

\c{bde} fails without changing anything if the schema is outside its range:

\
error: unable to migrate database bde: database schema version 1
is too old to migrate
error: unable to migrate database bde: database schema version 3
is newer than 2
\

An old schema is migrated in steps, first with an intermediate \c{bde}
version that still supports it. The release notes (the \c{NEWS} file) say
when a version drops the support for a schema. A newer schema means the
database belongs to a newer \c{bde}. To go back to an earlier \c{bde}, we
reinstall it and restore the backup made before the migration, which loses
the changes made since.

\N|All the \c{bde} processes that share a database have to use the same
schema version. So we stop all of them before the migration and only start
the upgraded ones after it.|

\h1#develop|Development|

This chapter is for the \c{bde} developers and assumes the development setup
from the repository's \c{README.md}.

\h#develop-test|Testing with PostgreSQL|

The \c{libbde} tests use the in-memory stores unless we give them a
PostgreSQL maintenance database (normally \c{postgres}) on a server where our
user may create databases. The setup script grants this permission to the
user we name with \c{--test-role}:

\
$ bde/etc/private/postgresql/bde-postgresql-setup --test-role \"$USER\"
$ PGHOST=/var/run/postgresql \
  bdep test config.libbde.test.pgsql=postgres
\

Each test creates its own database, named \c{bde_test_} followed by a random
suffix, and drops it once done. The tests connect with the \c{libpq}
defaults, so the \c{PGHOST} environment variable names the server's socket
directory (see \l{#install Installation}), and together with \c{PGPORT} it
can also select another server.

\h#develop-schema|Changing Database Schema|

The persistent classes are in \c{libbde/libbde/model.hxx}. The development
build (\c{config.libbde.develop=true}) translates this header with the ODB
compiler and copies the generated code to \c{libbde/libbde/pregenerated/},
where the consumption build takes it from. So after changing the header we
build in a development configuration and commit the regenerated code along
with the header. The \c{model} test fails if the two are out of sync.

The schema versions are defined in the same header:

\
#define LIBBDE_SCHEMA_VERSION_BASE 1
#define LIBBDE_SCHEMA_VERSION      1, open
\

The current version is \i{open} while its changes are unreleased, and the
changes made in the meantime fold into it. The ODB compiler records the closed
versions in the changelog, \c{libbde/libbde/model.xml}, and generates the
migration statements from it. The changelog is both its input and its output,
so we commit it as well. A schema version goes through the following steps:

\dl|

\li|\b{1. Close Version}\n

Before releasing an \c{bde} version with schema changes, we remove \c{open}
from \c{LIBBDE_SCHEMA_VERSION}, build, and commit the updated changelog.|

\li|\n\b{2. Open Next Version}\n

After the release, we bump the current version (for example, to
\c{2,\ open}) for the changes that follow.|

\li|\n\b{3. Raise Base Version}\n

To drop the migration from the oldest versions, we raise
\c{LIBBDE_SCHEMA_VERSION_BASE}. The deployments with an older schema then
migrate in steps (see \l{#upgrade-schema Migrating Database Schema}), so the
release notes have to say so.||

See the Database Schema Evolution chapter of the ODB manual for the changes
that the migration supports.
"
